Sure, loops are used to repeat a block of code multiple times.  In C, a loop can be written using the keyword "for", "while", or "do-while".  For example, the following code uses the "for" loop to print out the numbers from 1 to 10:

for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    cout << i << " ";
}

This code will print out the numbers 1 through 10, one at a time.  The "i" variable will be set to 1, then 2, then 3, and so on, until it reaches 10.  The "i <= 10" part of the loop test ensures that the loop runs only up to 10.  If you want to run the loop as many times as you need, you can just change the "i <= 10" part to "i < 10".

